--TEST--
`Core\Html::escape` replaces a directional control left open at the end of the text with `U+FFFD`, while a balanced pair passes through untouched — `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`, `rule:security/bidi-predicate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`'s last bullet, which is the row a reader does not expect on an
// escaper: escaping `<`, `>`, `&` and the quotes says nothing about display
// *order*, so a payload that reverses the rendering of everything after it
// survives the escape intact. `rule:security/bidi-predicate` owns the predicate; this member is its
// third caller, after the lexer's source spans and the terminal sink.

// Balanced — RLE opens a scope and PDF closes it inside the same value. This
// is legitimate mixed-direction text, and `rule:security/bidi-predicate`'s whole position is that
// banning the code points outright breaks Arabic and Hebrew.
string $balanced = "left \u{202B}right\u{202C} left again";
echo Core\Html::toSource(Core\Html::escape($balanced), "comparing the escape against its input") == $balanced
     ? "same" : "changed", "\n";

// Unterminated — RLO with no PDF after it, so the scope is still open when the
// value ends and everything downstream of it renders reversed. The control
// becomes `U+FFFD`: it is being removed, not displayed, so it is not written
// as `&#8238;` either, which would be an escaped payload rather than a
// neutralized one.
string $open = "safe\u{202E}reversed";
echo Core\Html::escape($open), "\n";

// Both bounds named together, so a member that neutralized every directional
// control rather than the unterminated ones fails on the first line here while
// still printing the right answer on the second.
echo Core\Html::toSource(Core\Html::escape($open), "comparing the escape against its input") == $open
     ? "same" : "changed", "\n";

// The two transformations compose in one pass: the escape and the
// substitution are decided per character over the same walk, so a value
// needing both gets both.
echo Core\Html::escape("a<b\u{2066}c"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
same
safe�reversed
changed
a&lt;b�c
